{"qid":"Q77","names":["Oriental Republic of Uruguay"],"title":"Oriental Republic of Uruguay","endonym":{"native":"República Oriental del Uruguay","lang":"es"},"capsule":"Uruguay (República Oriental del Uruguay) gained independence from Brazil in 1828 following the Cisplatine War and a convention brokered by Britain, which recognised the country as a buffer state between Argentina and Brazil. Through the nineteenth century Uruguay was convulsed by the Colorado-Blanco civil conflict (Guerra Grande, 1839–1851), but by the early twentieth century it emerged as one of Latin America's most progressive democracies under President José Batlle y Ordóñez (in office 1903–1907 and 1911–1915), who enacted universal male suffrage, women's rights, free secular education, and an early welfare state. A civic-military dictatorship ruled from 1973 to 1985, after which constitutional democracy was restored; Uruguay has since maintained high rankings in regional indexes of democracy, press freedom, and social development.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. National borders are precisely attested by treaties and surveyed boundaries; no significant territorial disputes affect the polygon.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Uruguay","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Uruguay"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Uruguay","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uruguay"}],"britannica":"place/Uruguay","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Uruguay","rulers":[{"qid":"Q125743","name":"Carlos Anaya","start":1834,"end":1835,"image":"Carlos Anaya.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Carlos%20Anaya.jpg?width=320","i